  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 122 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nCurrent analysis shows the closed low continuing to spin and move\nslowly east into western Wyoming this afternoon. Downstream of\nthis area of low pressure much of eastern Wyoming and western\nSouth Dakota sit under weak flow and widespread low clouds. These\nclouds have allowed the temperatures to remain steady, not\nchanging much from the overnight low temperatures as most areas\nare still sitting in the low 60s with a few pockets of mid to\nupper 60s.\n\nHeading through the rest of today, we will watch the Black Hills\nand areas to the west and south for potential thunderstorm\ninitiation this afternoon ahead of the main area of low pressure \nas it will continue to move east toward eastern Wyoming. Limited \nheating today may suppress more robust thunderstorm cores from \ndeveloping today but we are still expecting some thunderstorms to \ndevelop as the better dynamics associated with the main area of \nlow pressure continues to approach the area. Window for initiation \nlooks to be now (1pm MDT) through the next hour or so. The \ncoverage and strength of the thunderstorms will alter both the \nmarginal severe risk and excessive rainfall risk at least through\nSaturday. Still any storm that does develop will produce moderate\nto rainfall as they will be slow moving and will be able to tap \ninto the modest PWAT values sitting across western SD. \n\nCome Sunday we expect the coverage of showers with embedded\nthunderstorms to increase as the area of low pressure moves into\nthe region. Nearly everyone should see rainfall but totals will\ndepend on your proximity to the axis of where the clustering of\nthunderstorms are expected to be. Probabilities continue to  of \nseeing rainfall amounts over 1\" over the Black Hills and adjacent \nareas. Outside of these areas we could still rainfall totals close\nto 0.50\". Sunday will begin cloudy and foggy in most areas with\nmist/drizzle occurring before transitioning into rain. This rain\nwill persist through much of the day before we begin to slowly dry\nout from west to east come Sunday night before we finally get rid\nof the unsettled weather by Monday. This will begin the gradual\nwarm and dry pattern that will develop as a ridge of high pressure\nbegins to expand across the Intermountain West. Temperatures will\nclimb back to or slightly over seasonal normals by Wednesday.",
